{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>Let there be lights.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): the word יהי is in singular form because the light of both of them is but one, and accordingly it immediately says והיו למאורות — 'and they shall be for lights' — למארות is written (without the vav); or it refers back to the light created on the first day, for it should have said יהיו מאורות, but the intent is: let that light become lights, that is, let lights spread forth from it below." "<b>And according to the view</b> of the Ramban, of blessed memory, it appears that that light illuminated among the elements first, and when the firmament was made on the second day it intervened in the light and prevented it from illuminating the lower elements, and when the earth was created on the third day darkness lay over it, because the firmament formed a barrier between it and the light. And now on the fourth day the holy utterance decreed that lights should spread forth from that light, so that the light would reach the earth — and this is what it says: להאיר על הארץ, 'to give light upon the earth,' for it had been illuminating only in the firmament, and the illumination did not reach the earth. From this it is explained that night precedes day, as it also says above: ויהי ערב ויהי בקר." "<b>In the firmament of the heavens.</b> Scripture here calls the seven heavens, in which are the seven wandering stars, together with the eighth in which are all the stars and the twelve constellations, by the name רקיע, and it attributed this firmament to the heavens, which is the encompassing sphere, for they are its firmament — enclosed within it, moving with its motion. And it explained that all the lights are placed in these firmaments that are attributed to the heavens, and this is the sense of ברקיע השמים." "<b>And they shall be for signs.</b> For through the motion of the lights and their course, signs and portents are made in the heavens — blood and fire and columns of smoke — as the Sages, of blessed memory, interpreted: when the lights are stricken it is a bad sign for the nations of the world, as it is said (Jeremiah 10): ומאותות השמים אל תחתו. Furthermore, they are signs for Israel regarding the recitation of Shema of the morning, whose obligation is at sunrise, and the recitation of Shema of the evening, whose obligation is with the emergence of the stars. Also regarding the obligation of Birkat HaChamah at the solstice of Tammuz, when one is obligated to bless 'Blessed be the Maker of creation.' And so the Sages, of blessed memory, interpreted in Berakhot, chapter HaRo'eh: the Sages taught — one who sees the sun at its cycle and the moon in its purity and the stars in their stations and the constellations at their time says, 'Blessed be the Maker of creation.' And also when the moon is in its renewal, one is obligated to bless 'Who by His word created the heavens.'" "<b>And for seasons,</b> for sowing and harvest, cold and heat, summer and winter; <b>and for days,</b> the measure of day and the measure of night; <b>and years.</b> They complete their course through the twelve constellations in every year, and they return and begin to circle the sphere in the path they traversed." "<b>And the great master Rabbenu Moses bar Nahman, of blessed memory, wrote:</b> it is possible that just as He placed in the earth the power of vegetation in certain places within it, so too He placed in the firmament prepared and ready places to receive illumination, and these bodies receive light and shine; therefore He calls them מאורות — luminaries — and not אורים — lights — even though the Psalmist called them thus, end of his words." "<b>And it is possible</b> to interpret יהי מאורות — that these luminaries are not of the category of the spheres, but they are spiritual luminaries that receive illumination from the First Light. And He now decreed by this utterance יהי מאורות — that there shall extend from that First Light spiritual luminaries that shall be luminaries for the luminaries that are upon the earth. And this is what the verse says: ״והיו למאורות ברקיע השמים״ — 'and they shall be for luminaries in the firmament of the heavens'; it should have said ״והיו לרקיע השמים״ — 'and they shall be for the firmament of the heavens' — just as it said ״והיו לאותות״ — 'and they shall be for signs.' But the intent is that from the First Light, which is luminaries, they shall be luminaries for the luminaries upon the earth; for the verse did not say יהי מאורות ברקיע השמים להאיר על הארץ — 'let there be luminaries in the firmament of the heavens to illuminate the earth.' If so, understand that the first luminaries mentioned in יהי מאורות are spiritual luminaries that receive their light from the First Light and pour it forth into the luminaries below — and they are the delights of the souls of the righteous, who fulfill the Torah and the commandments. And this is what is said: ״והיו לאותות״ — that is, those luminaries shall be for signs for those who fulfilled the letters (אותיות) of the Torah, that they may merit and be delighted in them; or for those who fulfilled commandments in which an אות (sign) is mentioned, such as circumcision, Sabbath, and tefillin. And what the Psalmist called them (Psalms 136:7): ״לעושה אורים גדולים״ — 'to Him who makes great lights (אורים)' — the verse calls all of them מאורות, both the spiritual luminaries and the physical ones. Behold, David, peace be upon him, intended with the word אורים the spiritual ones, and this is why he afterward mentioned the lower luminaries, which are the sun and the moon, and said (Psalms 136): ״את השמש לממשלת ביום״ and so on, ״את הירח וכוכבים״ and so on. And even though the אורים receive illumination from the First Light, he called them אורים because they are the very essence of illumination compared to the sun and the moon. And it says ״ולמועדים״ — 'and for seasons' — because the soul, upon its separation from the body, does not immediately merit that great delight prepared for it until its appointed time (מועד) and its fixed season arrive, in the manner that the Sages, of blessed memory, expounded concerning that launderer who went up to the roof and fell and died: a bat kol went forth and said, 'This launderer is prepared for the life of the World to Come.' For the World of Souls comes to a person immediately after death, but the World to Come has a known appointed time and a fixed season, and they are distinct levels, each one in and of itself. There is the life of the World to Come at the time of the resurrection of the dead, and there is the World to Come after the resurrection, as I am destined to explain, with the help of Heaven, in the section אתם נצבים." "<b>And for days and years.</b> These are the times allotted to the ascendencies of the souls. For the soul ascends from level to level: from This World, which is the world of bodies, to the World of Souls; and from the World of Souls it ascends to the World of Spirits, which is called צרור (binding), as ״הנשמות צרורו שם״ — 'the souls are bound up there' — and it is called אור (light), in the manner of what is said (Job 33): ״וחיתו באור תראה״, and it is written (ibid.): ״לאור באור החיים״. And from the World of Spirits the soul ascends to the World of Life, from which comes the light, as is said (Psalms 36:10): ״כי עמך מקור חיים באורך נראה אור״. And whoever is not worthy to lengthen days in the World of Life but is worthy to receive measure for measure in the world of bodies — returns to live in the resurrection of the dead, which is from the force of the World of Life; therefore it says (Daniel 12:2): ״ורבים מישני אדמת עפר יקיצו״ — and not all those who sleep. And whoever is worthy to lengthen days in the World of Life has no interruption; and the World of Life is called נצח (eternity), as it is written (Psalms 16:11): ״תודיעני אורח חיים שובע שמחות את פניך נעימות בימינך נצח״. And from this you have learned that the life of the preceding worlds and the supernal levels, which He built into the מאורות, are the delights of the souls that merit the Torah, and they shall merit those levels at the times known to them — these being days and these being years — as they hinted in this verse: ״ולימים ושנים״ — 'and for days and years' — because their soul ascends from level to level. And this matter is further elucidated from what He afterward mentioned in the account of the deed, repeating the matter: ״ויעש אלהים את שני המאורות הגדולים״ — these are the spiritual ones; ״את המאור הגדול ואת המאור הקטן״ — these are the physical ones, and they are the sun (חמה) and the moon (לבנה)." "<b>And by way of</b> the plain sense (peshat): the reason He called both of them 'great' is that each one of them is great and mighty in its own right; and afterward ״את המאור הגדול ואת המאור הקטן״ — because every great thing is small in relation to something greater than it. <b>Know that</b> if you examine this passage closely, four matters will become clear to you from it. The first: that these luminaries are always in those places where they were first fixed — that is, they are fixed and do not move of themselves; rather, the sphere is what moves, and the luminaries are fixed within it and move with its motion, like the nails fixed in the wall of a ship that move with the motion of the ship. And in this the Sages of Israel conceded to the Sages of the nations of the world: for the Sages of Israel say 'the sphere is fixed and the star returns,' and the Sages of the nations say 'the star is fixed and the sphere returns.' Concerning this it says ״ויתן אותם אלהים״ — 'and Elohim set them' — go out and learn who is the One who sets them, may His great name be blessed and exalted. The second: that they are fixed within the thickness of the body of the spheres, for the thickness of the body of the sphere is filled by the vastness of the body of the luminaries; and concerning this it says ״ברקיע השמים״ — 'in the firmament of the heavens.' And so David said (Psalms 19:5): ״לשמש שם אהל בהם״ — its meaning is 'in one of them,' that is, among the heavens mentioned, which are the spheres, and it is the fourth sphere. For every firmament is a tent for the star fixed within it, and the size of the star is like the thickness of the firmament, and the thickness of the firmament is the measure of the distance from heaven to earth, and all of it is five hundred years. And this is learned from the verse that Deborah said (Judges 5:20): ״מן שמים נלחמו הכוכבים ממסלותם נלחמו עם סיסרא״ — elucidating that the measure of the stars in their courses, which are the firmaments, is like the measure from the heavens down to Sisera; and so Rashi, of blessed memory, explained. The third: that the thickness of the spheres does not prevent the light of the luminaries and stars from reaching below, for the very essence of the heavens is clear and pure; and this is what it says ״להאיר על הארץ״ — that the illumination should reach the earth, and the thickness of the spheres does not intervene. The fourth: that the light of the moon is drawn from the light of the sun, for the moon has no light from itself but only from the light of the sun; and from this the Sages, of blessed memory, established in the prayer of יוצר: ״ראה והתקין צורת הלבנה״ — that is, that it is a form without illumination. And what the Sages, of blessed memory, call 'the light of the moon' when they say 'they do not inspect, neither the light of the sun nor the light of the moon' — they say this concerning the light emanated upon it from the sun, and it is like the heat of iron that has been heated in fire: the heat is not from the iron itself but from the fire that heats it, and the iron receives the heat from it. And concerning this it says ״והיו למאורות״ — defective, written למארת without a vav, for the two of them are one light. And so the Sages, of blessed memory, expounded: 'It was not created to illuminate but the sphere of the sun alone' — and this is the moon's complaint, when it said: 'It is impossible for two kings to use one crown' — for 'crown' is a term for light and radiance. And a proof from Scripture that it has no light from itself is what the Holy One, blessed be He, said to Job (Job 36 — should be 38): ״בשומי ענן לבושו וערפל חתולתו״. And since the garment of the firmament that is above our heads and closest to us is the sphere of the moon, and it is what covers and hovers over the sea, and it is what moves the element of water, therefore He called the moon 'cloud' (ענן) and 'mist' (ערפל), because it is dark and has no light in itself; and He said that it is 'its garment' (לבושו) and 'its swaddling-band' (חתולתו) of the day."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {8 "The citation (איוב לו) for בשומי ענן לבושו וערפל חתולתו points to Job 36, but the verse is Job 38:9. The Hebrew reads חתולתו (swaddling-band) for the MT's חתולתו (same word); note also that the verse contextually concerns the cloud as garment of the sea, not of the moon. Rendered hyper-literally; the possible conflation of referents is left as in the source."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T23:25:46.936359222Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}